WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:04.500
Times are tough, jobs are scarce.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.640
It's risky to be outside
and around others.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.390
It seems like there's very few ways

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:10.373
to make extra money during a pandemic.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:12.681
So I guess I'll just hunker down

00:00:12.681 --> 00:00:17.681
and binge social media
until this all blows over.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.683
Wait, don't these people make money?

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.390
How do these people make money?

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.290
Streamers, YouTubers, Twitch
stars, how do they make money?

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:40.270
Is it a lot?

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:41.760
Is it a lot of work?

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.340
I know, I got to ask somebody in the biz

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.123
and I got just the guy, Austin John Plays.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:50.520
This is your full-time job now, right?

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.080
As of November of last year, yes.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.480
This was always just kind
of like a side thing.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.550
Like any money that I made

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:00.980
from doing my videos and whatever else,

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:03.160
I never touched that 'cause
I didn't really think

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:04.710
of that as being like real money.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:06.980
And then it started
coming in more and more.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.000
And then it was like, I hit my first day

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.660
that I broke a grand and I was like,

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.840
this is actually gonna be money.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.500
How did the YouTube channel come to be?

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:21.410
When the game Pokemon Sun
and Moon came out for the 3DS

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:23.900
there was like things that
I learned about the game.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.720
And then I wanted to just share

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.276
that information with people.

00:01:27.276 --> 00:01:30.270
And at the time I didn't have
any way to capture a screen

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:32.770
or anything so I found
like generic footage

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:34.350
and I kind of put it all together

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:39.158
with so much editing and
that video got six views.

00:01:39.158 --> 00:01:41.720
And then two days later I made another one

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.250
and that one got 12 views.

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:46.910
And then I just started pumping
out more and more content.

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:47.743
And people were like,

00:01:47.743 --> 00:01:49.370
this guy knows what he's talking about.

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:51.620
It really just started
building on top of itself.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.280
And then it was like.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.300
My channel really focuses
on tips and tricks.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.426
So I play a straight 18 hours

00:01:58.426 --> 00:02:02.370
and I'll finish the game or
get to about the 40 hour mark

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.770
and then I'll make my first video.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.110
While I'm going through
I have a giant notepad

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.920
and just scribbles everywhere.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:11.751
And I make these notes of like
the things that stumped me.

00:02:11.751 --> 00:02:15.136
So I know the things that
may stump other people.

00:02:15.136 --> 00:02:17.190
What do you need to think about

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:19.270
if you're building your YouTube channel?

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:21.370
One, you need to do something

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:23.250
that you're passionate about.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.083
Not that you like,

00:02:24.083 --> 00:02:26.150
not that you think you're
going to make money on,

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:28.230
something you're passionate about.

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:29.210
Other thing is something

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.340
that other people are passionate about.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:33.780
It holds a special place to them.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.670
And third is, it needs to be something

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.500
that people are going to
seek out information on.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:42.280
In order to enter into the marketplace,

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:46.120
you either need to be
better than everyone else,

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.080
you either need to be
faster than everyone else

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.470
or you need to be more
accessible than everyone else.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:53.740
And you can't be more accessible

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.150
'cause it's already on the platform.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:57.290
Granted, if you understand
how the algorithm works,

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:00.010
which is very similar to
Google Analytics, then yeah,

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:01.909
you can have a little bit of a headstart,

00:03:01.909 --> 00:03:03.990
but if you're not doing it faster

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:05.700
and if you're not doing it
better than everyone else

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.580
then why should anyone watch your channel?

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.300
So my income as a YouTuber
kind of breaks down

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.930
to three different ways.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:17.610
One, which is the bulk of
it, which is ad revenue.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.990
Whenever you go and you watch a video

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:22.490
and then there's an ad and
then you wait five seconds

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:23.830
and you hit the bottom right corner

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:26.860
and then you go onto the next
video or a non-skippable ad

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:29.120
or the video starts immediately,

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:30.810
you get the little
banner ads at the bottom.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:34.170
Or if you scroll down from the video

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:36.070
the first thing that shows there is an ad.

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:37.840
Those are the four different types of ads

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.439
that can appear on every
single YouTube video,

00:03:40.439 --> 00:03:43.580
and all of them pay a certain amount.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:47.051
It varies on lots of
things like the country

00:03:47.051 --> 00:03:48.920
of the person who posted,

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:52.620
the country of the person who
watches it, the time of year,

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.040
where you are in the quarter,
if there's anything going

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:58.680
on with a reduction in ad revenue.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:02.380
Like great example, COVID-19
companies weren't spending

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:07.370
as much on advertising so
because of that, CPM went down.

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:10.930
CPM stands for click per thousand

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:13.890
and using the Roman numeral for 1,000.

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:16.040
And that means that for every 1,000 views

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:17.650
you get a certain amount of money.

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:20.560
Now on low ads, when I first started off,

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.470
my CPM was 30 cents.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:26.270
So every 1,000 views, I made 30 cents,

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:28.590
but then once I signed with an MCN

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:30.770
and I started making more quality content

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:34.230
and more engaging content,
some of my better videos

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:35.580
and certain times of the year

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.763
I can see CPMs as high as 12 to $14.

00:04:40.035 --> 00:04:43.000
And there's a big
fluctuation between, you know

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.550
if I post a video when
there's no ads being spent

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:48.420
like January and February.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.510
That's a reason a lot of
your favorite YouTubers

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:55.280
and also TV shows don't
post new videos in that time

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.110
of year 'cause ad rates are down.

00:04:57.110 --> 00:04:58.510
Why are they gonna make content then?

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:00.550
Instead you're recording
and you're bulking up

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:02.810
for when ad revenue is higher.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:07.810
The second way that a YouTuber
makes income is support,

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:09.960
crowdfunding, things like that.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:13.840
When I first started off, Patreon
was really the only option

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.160
but then they started
rolling out supporters

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:17.980
and members for YouTube channels.

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:20.240
So whenever I have a live stream

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:22.460
you can click a button and
then you can become a member.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:25.150
And then you get special
icons next to your name.

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:27.500
I believe they're called
badges or on any video

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:28.760
you can hit the join button

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:30.910
and that helps support the channel.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:34.120
And then the third aspect
of that would be merch.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:37.000
And for me, my merch
has been somewhat basic.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.010
In fact, I'm wearing one of my T-shirts

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:40.704
from last year right now.

00:05:40.704 --> 00:05:43.660
You have to endure the
times that your first video

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:46.604
gets six views, then you post
it on Reddit and they say,

00:05:46.604 --> 00:05:49.827
shut up and then an auto mod bans you.

00:05:49.827 --> 00:05:51.430
You have to go through that

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:54.134
because until you go through that

00:05:54.134 --> 00:05:56.950
and you discover why you're doing it,

00:05:56.950 --> 00:05:58.370
you're not doing it for other people.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:05:59.870
You're doing it for yourself.

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:04.053
If you're not loving what you
do, then it's not worth it.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:08.160
Huh, so make a channel

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.300
about something that I'm passionate about.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:12.303
Oh, sweet.

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:16.053
I guess I can make the channel about that.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:35.594
Now, how do I grow an audience?

00:06:35.594 --> 00:06:38.673
How do I get popular?

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:42.410
It's all about diversification, right?

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:44.870
You have to have a
massive Twitter following.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:46.960
You have to have a massive
Instagram following.

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:49.330
You have to have a
massive YouTube following.

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:50.850
Of course you have to be on Twitch,

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:54.486
which is the key platform, but
basically any way you can get

00:06:54.486 --> 00:06:58.717
in front of your audience, you
can engage with your audience

00:06:58.717 --> 00:07:01.370
is going to drive those clicks

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:03.810
which ultimately drives
the advertising revenue

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:06.590
that you get back from
all of these platforms.

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:08.520
It's me and Dan playing video games

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.660
and talking in a funny manner over them.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:13.050
They're not just people
who are good at games.

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:16.460
They are actually people who
are good at online engagement,

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:18.832
online entertainment first and foremost.

00:07:18.832 --> 00:07:20.170
You have the right to remain stylish.

00:07:20.170 --> 00:07:22.062
Anything you wear can and
will be used against you

00:07:22.062 --> 00:07:23.570
in the court of fashion.

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:28.150
The streamer is either very,
very talented at the game.

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:30.490
The best player in the game

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:32.090
is generally going to draw a crowd

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:34.430
'cause people like watching
people who are very good.

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:36.880
On the other hand, they're
either very, very good

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:41.620
or funny, charismatic,
whatever it is on that side.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:45.084
Oh, no.

00:07:45.084 --> 00:07:47.380
You know, it becomes more
of a show where they talk

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:49.827
about other things outside
of the gaming world.

00:07:49.827 --> 00:07:51.911
And gaming becomes really just a backdrop

00:07:51.911 --> 00:07:54.370
and an excuse to engage
with that audience.

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:56.480
There were those weird
like Ronald McDonald,

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:59.490
like straight to VHS
movies, you remember those?

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:00.840
I'm personable, I'm chatty.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.150
I would kind of, you know, when
boring things were happening

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:05.020
in the game, I would
ramble on about, you know

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:07.510
movies I'd watched or
books I'd read on, you know

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:09.507
or conversations I'd
had and stuff like that.

00:08:09.507 --> 00:08:12.072
And so I think being personable
was a big part of that.

00:08:12.072 --> 00:08:14.895
It's not just getting
on and playing games.

00:08:14.895 --> 00:08:17.470
It's getting on and being an entertainer.

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:19.640
You are putting on a show.

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:20.810
All right now, let me go ahead

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:23.618
and grab the samurai
sword that every gym has.

00:08:23.618 --> 00:08:25.230
Okay, he's dead.

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:29.200
To have a larger audience
and to have that audience,

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:32.125
I think, stay with you, you
have to bring something extra.

00:08:32.125 --> 00:08:34.330
You know, you have to find your niche.

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:36.630
You have to find what
works and what connects

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:38.430
with the audience and
what makes them laugh

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:40.023
or what makes them keep coming back.

00:08:40.023 --> 00:08:43.830
You gotta make it a little
more exciting, all right?

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:45.100
Throw in a hanging Bishop,

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:47.123
have everybody in suspense for a moment.

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:51.950
I think the real, most important thing is,

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:53.750
is to just get used to being on camera.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:55.910
Like in any sort of performance thing

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:58.113
is get used to talking on camera.

00:08:58.113 --> 00:09:01.322
You know, my first few
videos were terrible

00:09:01.322 --> 00:09:03.270
but because I sat down and did it

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:06.040
for six to eight hours a day,
I just got used to talking

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:08.335
and I got over that uncomfortable hump.

00:09:08.335 --> 00:09:12.260
If you go into Twitch
purely with the expectation

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:16.290
that you're going to make
money, make a job out of it,

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:18.962
have all this growth and
it's going to be great,

00:09:18.962 --> 00:09:21.675
you're probably going to
burn out prior to getting

00:09:21.675 --> 00:09:24.790
to that point because
there's very little payoff

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:26.320
for a long time, right?

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:29.233
Like, multiple years generally

00:09:29.233 --> 00:09:32.200
before you really start to see a payoff.

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:34.570
And so, you know, those first few times

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:36.940
you're streaming, nobody is there.

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:39.590
One person shows up every 30 minutes,

00:09:39.590 --> 00:09:41.510
says hi maybe and leaves, right?

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:46.510
Like, it's very discouraging,
I think, for a lot of people.

00:09:46.717 --> 00:09:50.370
And so you have to be
there just loving it.

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:54.385
You have to love talking to
yourself when nobody is around

00:09:54.385 --> 00:09:57.250
you have to love just the
game that you're playing,

00:09:57.250 --> 00:10:00.770
all of that stuff
because it takes a while.

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:03.760
And I think a majority of people burn out

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:07.093
prior to getting to the
profitable point of it.

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:14.820
And there you have it, my
very own YouTube, Twitch,

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:17.730
Discord, and streaming
channels all ready to go live.

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:20.360
It's a veritable social media ecosystem

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:22.902
just on the verge of thriving.

00:10:22.902 --> 00:10:26.400
So don't forget to
smash like and subscribe

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:28.590
and visit my Patreon for perks.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:30.920
Also, go to my merch page.

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:34.053
And one last thing, stay cool, my friends.

